WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.420
Changes in your diet not
only affect you physically,

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.400
physiologically inside,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.160
but also mentally,
how well you think,

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.680
psychologically,
how well you feel.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.740
But you’ll never know just
how good you can feel

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.600
until you put it to the test
and try eating healthy.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.880
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.158
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:24.158 --> 00:00:27.960
Today we look at the surprising
versatility of fecal transplants.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.320
Did you know they can be used
to treat clinical conditions

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.880
like ulcerative colitis,
depression,

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.220
bipolar disorder,
and alcoholism?

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.457
Here’s the story.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.380
Plant-based foods and the microbiome

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.320
in the preservation of health
and prevention of disease.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.740
We have evidence that high-fiber,
plant-based diets can prevent

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.160
many different common diseases,
maybe through the effect

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.400
that these diets have on the
composition and metabolic activity

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.700
of our microbiome,
the bacteria in our gut.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.760
Good gut bugs in our colon
eat the plant residues and

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.000
spit out health-promoting and
cancer-suppressing metabolites.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.180
Like fiber is metabolized
to short-chain fatty acids,

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.080
which have profound

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.360
anti-inflammatory,
anti-cancer properties.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.780
And we have special fiber-feeding
microbes that chew through

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.360
the plant cell walls and release
all the anti-inflammatory,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.820
antioxidant, and
anticancer goodies inside.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.920
So, how many whole plant foods
do we have to eat?

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.860
All the evidence points to
a physiological need

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.020
for about 50 grams
of fiber a day,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.260
which is what is contained
in the traditional African diet

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.640
and is associated with the prevention
of common Western chronic diseases.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.940
This is up to twice
the recommended minimum

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.940
and three times the current
intake in the United States.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.720
How can you prove the microbiome
is involved though?

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.380
Fecal transplantation --
stool transplants.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.220
One man's trash,
another man's treasure.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:58.920
Currently, donor fecal microbiota
transplantation is the optimum

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.460
therapeutic approach for recurrent
Clostridium difficile infection.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.320
C. diff is today considered the most
common hospital-acquired cause

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.120
of diarrhea. It's a life-threatening
infection that can rear its ugly head

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.180
when your good gut bugs have been
wiped out by antibiotics.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.200
So to get rid of it, all we need
are more good gut bugs,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.260
which can be provided
by a healthy donor

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.160
through the infusion of a liquid
suspension of the donor's stool.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.980
We are winning with poo.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.140
Fecal transplants have proven
their worth in the management

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.680
of recurrent C. diff diarrhea,
with cure rates up to 90%.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.520
Because of this success, fecal
transplants have been suggested

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.640
as a potential treatment in other
gastrointestinal diseases.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.780
For example, the inflammatory bowel
disease known as ulcerative colitis.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.360
There have been four
randomized controlled trials,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.260
and fecal transplants appear to nearly
double clinical remission rates.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.160
But what about
non-gastrointestinal disorders?

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.160
Here's a case study
of a series of fecal transplants

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.840
for multiple sclerosis (MS).

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.840
The patient experienced improvements
in their microbiome,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.660
more of those fiber-feeding
anti-inflammatory bacteria.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.680
And there was a significant increase
in the levels of brain derived

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.260
neurotrophic factor,
which is suggested to play

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.860
a neuroprotective role in MS,
as well as

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.280
both subjective and objective
evidence of improved walking,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.160
which was the patient's
primary MS symptom.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.460
Now, to give the new fiber feeders
something to chew on,

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.400
the patient was put on
a high fiber diet, which is great.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.180
But just putting people on
a plant-based diet alone may help,

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.920
stool transplants or not,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.980
since standard Western diets
promote dysbiosis,

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:45.300
a bad bug gut imbalance, and
neuroinflammation in MS,

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.540
while plant-based high-fiber
diets decrease MS risks.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:53.220
Randomized, placebo-controlled trials
will be needed to tease that out,

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.640
but in the meanwhile we can
certainly build up good gut bugs

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.360
the old-fashioned way by eating
fiber-rich whole plant foods.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.020
What about fecal transplants
for depression?

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:08.400
Interest in the gut microbiome
and its role in health has exploded

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.080
just in the last few years. Does any
of this have relevance in psychiatry?

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.520
Maybe so. Several studies
demonstrate microbiome differences

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.700
between depressed persons
and non-depressed persons.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.480
And after all, an imbalance in
neurotransmitters is implicated

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.340
in the cause of depression,

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.520
and the gut microbiome is known
to synthesize large quantities

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.900
of these same neuroactive substances,
like serotonin and dopamine.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.200
And there's a major information
highway from the gut to the brain

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.320
called the vagus nerve that could
potentially alter mood states.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:44.400
Early fecal transplants were
delivered from the bottom up,

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.080
through colonoscopy,
but now

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.200
we have crapsules -- encapsulated
feces to make it easier to swap stool.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:57.420
Here's a case report of a 79-year-old
woman who suffered a tragedy

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:01.020
and subsequently lost her appetite,
became introverted, drowsy,

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:02.400
and stayed in bed all day,

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.280
wasting away 55 pounds
over the next six months.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.920
She was hospitalized for
depression and prescribed

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.920
multiple anti-depressant drugs,
but to no avail.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:15.840
So, what the heck, they stopped
the drugs and tried a fecal transplant.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.280
The stool donor was her own
6-year-old great-grandson,

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.220
who had a good appetite,
an outgoing personality,

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:28.560
and a disciplinary stool --
I don't even know what that means.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.220
Four days after the fecal transplant,
she started feeling better,

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.980
and by two weeks
she was frankly euphoric.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.080
She was able to go back home,

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.780
and within six weeks
all was back to normal.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.460
What about a fecal transplant
for bipolar disorder?

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.960
The poor woman was in and out of
psych hospitals with manic depression

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.064
until she got a fecal transplant
with stool from her husband --

00:05:53.064 --> 00:05:54.120
that 's romantic --

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.880
and within six months
she was symptom free.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.500
This case report was published
in 2020, so symptom free for years,

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:06.120
no longer on medication, and has
lost more than 70 pounds to boot,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:11.100
either because she got slim hubby stool
or just being off her psych meds.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.680
She went from being
functionally disabled

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.920
to running a small business
and has published two books.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:21.480
What about alcohol dependence?
Alcoholism has traditionally

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:24.600
been considered exclusively
a brain disorder, but, hey,

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.960
all these other psychiatric syndromes
seem to have links to the gut.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:31.140
So they compared the guts
of alcoholics to nonalcoholics,

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:33.480
and they found that some
alcohol-dependent subjects

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.400
developed gut leakiness,

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.860
which was associated
with higher alcohol craving.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:41.400
Okay, but maybe instead
of the gut issues somehow

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:45.120
leading to alcohol cravings,
the alcohol led to the gut issues.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.080
Alcohol may have a toxic effect
on the gut wall, but both groups

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.360
were consuming the same
amount of alcohol at the time,

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.420
so maybe there is some kind
of gut connection after all.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:58.740
This was the study that
blew people's minds.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.920
The transplant of feces
from alcoholics

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.920
into mice induced
an alcohol preference.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.220
These were mice without any
previous contact with alcohol,

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.280
all of a sudden spontaneously
preferring alcohol.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:13.800
Normally mice don't
really like alcohol,

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.800
but feed them some feces
from an alcoholic human,

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.280
and they do
a 180-degree switch.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:25.620
So wait. Are you saying that gut bugs
can determine alcohol craving?

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:30.900
There's only one way to find out,
and that's put it to the test.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:36.180
A randomized, double-blind, clinical
trial of fecal microbiota transplant

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:37.860
for alcohol use disorder,

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.020
which is the current clinical
term for alcoholism.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.540
They took alcoholics
with liver cirrhosis

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.180
and active problem drinking
and randomized them

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:49.200
to get either a placebo enema
or a fecal transplant enema.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.240
And within two weeks, alcohol
cravings reduced significantly

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.880
in 90% of the fecal transplant cases

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.280
versus just 30%
in placebo cases.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:03.480
And this was validated by pee tests
showing that they were drinking

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.620
significantly less too,

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:08.040
with improved cognition and
psychosocial quality of life.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:12.420
Now this is all still
just experimental,

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:14.520
and not without
potential downsides.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.300
Yes, they're working on ways to make
stool transplants more palatable.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.880
On balance, they've largely
been found to be safe,

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:27.000
though there have been cases
of bad bugs being transferred

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.460
from donors, even a fatal case.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:34.440
And there's even a theoretical risk
you could be transplanted

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.600
with cancer cells, making it

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:41.095
the gift that keeps on giving,
but in a bad way.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:42.420
In our next story,

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:46.680
we look at what’s more important:
probiotics or prebiotics?

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:48.878
And where can
we best get them?

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.720
Virtually every day,
we are all confronted

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:53.880
with the activity
of our intestine,

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:56.400
and it is no surprise that
at least some of us

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:59.460
have developed a fascination
with our intestinal condition

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:02.640
and its relation to
health and disease.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:06.720
Over the last years, the
intestinal microbiota,

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.420
our gut flora, has
been identified as like

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:13.620
a fascinating ‘new organ,’
with all sorts of functions.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:15.960
Well, if the bacteria in our gut

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.140
make up a whole separate
organ inside our body,

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:22.380
what about doing
an organ transplant?

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:27.720
What would happen if you
transferred intestinal bacteria

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:30.840
from lean people
into obese people?

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:35.640
Researchers figured that rebalancing
the obesity-causing bacteria

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.440
with an infusion
of gut bacteria

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.360
from a lean person
might help.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.500
Now, they wanted this to
be a placebo-controlled study,

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.840
which for drugs is easy:
give a sugar pill.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.600
But, when you’re sticking
a tube down people’s throats

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:50.280
and transplanting feces,

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:54.000
I’m thinkin’ what do you
use as a poop placebo?

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.640
A poop-cebo, if you will!

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:01.740
Both the donors and the subjects
brought in fresh stools,

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.420
and the subjects
were randomized

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:04.860
to either get
the donor’s stool

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:08.220
or get transplanted with
their own collected feces—

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:11.580
that was the placebo, you
just get your own back.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:13.920
Okay, so what happened?

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:17.700
The insulin sensitivity
of the skinny donors

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:19.560
was up around 50;
that’s a good thing.

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:23.640
High insulin sensitivity means
low insulin resistance,

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:26.040
the cause of both type 2
diabetes and prediabetes.

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:29.880
The obese subjects
started out around 20,

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:32.220
and after an infusion
of their own feces,

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.380
they stayed
around 20.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:38.100
But, the group of obese
donors getting the skinny

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:41.040
similarly started
low but shot up

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.200
near to where the
slim folks were.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:46.680
It’s interesting: not all
lean donor stools

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:49.560
conveyed the same effect
on insulin sensitivity,

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:52.560
as some donors had
very significant effects,

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.080
the so-called
super-fecal donor,

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.240
whereas others had
little or no effect.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:02.100
Turns out this super-donor effect
is most probably conveyed

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:04.320
by the amounts of short-chain
fatty acid-producing

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.480
intestinal bacteria
in their feces,

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:11.340
the food bacteria that thrive
off of the fiber we eat.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:13.560
The short chain
fatty acids produced

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:17.100
by fiber-eating bacteria may
contribute to the release

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:19.260
of gut hormones that
may be the cause

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:22.560
of this beneficial improved
insulin sensitivity.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:26.280
The successful use of
fecal transplantation

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.340
has recently attracted
considerable attention,

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:31.500
not only because
of its success,

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:35.580
but its capacity to prove
the cause and effect relationship

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.560
that the bacteria
we have in our gut

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:39.840
can affect
our metabolism.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.440
But, within a few months,
the bacterial composition

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.480
returned back to baseline;

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:48.720
so, the effects on the obese
subjects were temporary.

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:52.800
We can get similar benefits
though, by just feeding

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:56.040
what few good gut bacteria
we may already have.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:01.200
Say you have a shed
full of bunny rabbits.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.740
Feed them pork rinds,
and they all die.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:09.360
Yes, you can repopulate your shed
by infusing new bunnies,

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:12.540
but if you keep feeding
them pork rinds,

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:14.220
they’ll eventually
die off as well.

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:18.180
Whereas, even if you start off
with just a few bunnies,

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:20.340
if you feed them what
they’re meant to eat,

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.140
they’ll grow and
multiply and soon

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:25.500
you’ll be full of
fiber-eating bunnies.

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:31.320
Fecal transplants and probiotics
are only temporary fixes,

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.500
if we keep putting the
wrong fuel into our gut.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:39.300
But, by eating prebiotics,
such as fiber, which means

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:41.820
increasing whole
plant food consumption,

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:44.880
we may select for and
foster the growth

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:47.460
of our own good bacteria.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:50.940
However, such effects may abate

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:53.160
once the high-fiber
intake ceases.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:56.160
Therefore, our dietary habits,
should include

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:59.160
a continuous consumption
of large quantities

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.800
of high-fiber foods
to improve our health.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:08.534
And, if we don’t, we may be
starving our microbial self.

